<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # We Can Swim. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Time for Exercise</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> We Can Swim.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;" colspan="3">
            <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Talk about people or things using simple language. Interactive: Discuss homework assignments with peers.</p>
            <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can talk about people, using simple descriptions (e.g., "My friend can swim."). Can discuss homework assignments with peers by offering or requesting help and following up with clarifying questions (e.g., "I need help with my physical education homework." "I can help you with the instructions." "What part do you need help with?"). Can ask classmates about their homework (e.g., "What is the homework for our physical education class?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Simon Says" game using action verbs related to sports and exercise. Students listen to commands and perform the actions if "Simon Says." The teacher uses verbs like "swim," "run," "jump," "dance," "walk," "kick," "throw." Teacher encourages students to call out actions they like: "I like to dance!" or "I can jump!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using "can" and "cannot" with actions. For example, "I can swim. I cannot fly." (making a humorous gesture). The teacher clearly pronounces the /k/ sound in "can" and the /s/ sound in "swim" and exaggerates segmenting multisyllabic words like "exciting" (ex-cit-ing) by clapping for each syllable. The teacher demonstrates asking about preferences: "Do you like to swim?" and expressing preferences: "Yes, I do. I like swimming."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I can swim,' does it mean I go in the water?" (Expected answer: Yes!)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I cannot run fast,' does it mean I run very quickly?" (Expected answer: No!)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of someone swimming) "Is this 'running' or 'swimming'?" (Expected answer: Swimming!)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> The teacher presents a short, illustrated dialogue between two friends, Leo and Mia, discussing their favorite exercises and a physical education homework assignment. The dialogue includes target grammar (can/cannot, present simple questions, WH-questions with verb + ing) and vocabulary (swimming, running, cycling, friend, homework, exercise).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated dialogue printed on a large poster or displayed on a projector. The dialogue is conversational and includes speech bubbles. Example sentences: "Hi, Mia! Do you like sports?" "Yes, Leo, I do! I like swimming." "Oh, that's exciting! I can swim, but I like cycling every day." "What is the homework for our physical education class?" "It's about 'healthy activities.' I need help with the instructions for drawing." "I can help you with the instructions! What part do you need help with?" "Let's work on it together this afternoon."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Linguistic Competence: Sociolinguistic — Encouraging others: Suggesting group activities to encourage and support peers (e.g., "Let's run together!" "You can do it!"). Pragmatic — Asking about preferences: "Do you like to swim?" Expressing preferences: "I like swimming."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads the dialogue aloud, pointing to the speakers. Then, the teacher asks gist questions for students to answer orally in pairs, then share with the class:</p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What sports do Leo and Mia talk about?" (Expected: Swimming, cycling.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who can help with the homework?" (Expected: Mia can help Leo.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the homework about?" (Expected: Healthy activities.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Speaking.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using modal verbs (can, cannot), present simple questions and affirmative sentences (Do you like sports? Yes, I do. I ride my bike every day.), and WH-question + answer + subject + verb + ing (What type of exercise do you like? I like swimming.). Vocabulary: walk, exercise, afternoon, gym, sport, activity, friend, football, swimming, running, hiking, dancing, cycling, horseback riding; go, swim, walk, play, run, jump, throw, catch, kick, hit, dance, climb, hike; healthy, active, fit, fun, exciting; every day, always, sometimes. Pronunciation: /k/ in "can", /s/ in "swim", segmenting "wellness", "exciting."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Sentence Completion & Choral Repetition:</b> The teacher displays incomplete sentences related to abilities and preferences on the board. Students orally complete them using "can," "cannot," "like," or "likes." For example: "My friend ___ swim." "Do you ___ football?" "I ___ hiking." Students then repeat the completed sentences chorally, focusing on the /k/ and /s/ sounds. Teacher ensures clear segmentation of multi-syllabic words like "wellness" and "exciting" during repetition.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice – Picture Card Q&A:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of picture cards showing different activities (e.g., swimming, running, dancing, horseback riding). Student A picks a card and asks Student B: "Do you like [activity on card]?" Student B responds: "Yes, I do. I like [activity on card]." or "No, I don't. I don't like [activity on card]." Then, Student A asks: "What type of exercise do you like?" Student B answers: "I like [verb + ing]." Students switch roles after a few turns. Teacher circulates, providing feedback on pronunciation and grammar.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "My Classmate Can/Cannot":</b> Students work in new pairs. They get a simple checklist with various activities. Students interview their partner using "Can you [activity]?" and "Do you like [activity]?" They record their partner's answers by checking "can" or "cannot" and "likes" or "doesn't like." After the interview, each student orally reports to a new pair or the whole class about their partner, e.g., "My friend Alex can swim. He likes cycling. He cannot play football very well." Encourage use of "every day," "sometimes."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students engage in a role-play activity simulating a real-life scenario of discussing a physical education homework assignment and offering/requesting help.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Pair Role-Play. Students are divided into pairs. Each pair receives a scenario card (e.g., "You have P.E. homework about different sports, but you don't understand the instructions for 'cycling'. Ask your friend for help." or "Your friend needs help with P.E. homework about 'healthy activities'. Offer to help and ask clarifying questions.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students use language related to "Time for Exercise" and "We Can Swim." to discuss their P.E. homework. They must use the target grammar and vocabulary naturally. Teacher reminds students to use encouraging phrases like "You can do it!" or "Let's do it together!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read their scenario cards and prepare their dialogue in pairs for 3-5 minutes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pairs perform their role-play for another pair or for the class.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Key phrases to encourage: "What is the homework for our physical education class?" "I need help with my physical education homework." "I can help you with the instructions." "What part do you need help with?" "Do you like running?" "Let's run together!" "You can do it!"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during the role-play performance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the role-play in pairs, focusing on discussing homework and offering/requesting help.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., sport, activity, homework, swim, run, bike)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of "can" and "cannot":</b> (e.g., "I can help," "I cannot understand")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Asking/Expressing preferences:</b> (e.g., "Do you like...?", "I like...")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Asking for/offering help:</b> (e.g., "I need help," "I can help you")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> (Intelligibility of /k/ and /s/ sounds, segmenting multisyllabic words)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> (Ability to interact and respond to a peer)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small piece of paper before leaving:</p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I can talk about today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I still need help with..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b> (Teacher asks students to think about their performance and learning)</p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of talking about sports or homework was easy for you today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new word or phrase you used that you can remember for next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What speaking activities worked best to encourage student interaction and fluency? Were students able to use "can/cannot" and express preferences effectively? Was the pronunciation practice for /k/ and /s/ sufficient? What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., more sentence frames, specific role-play prompts) are needed for students struggling with spontaneous speaking? Were the sociolinguistic elements of encouragement well-integrated?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), students will use the vocabulary and grammar learned about sports and activities to write short descriptions about their favorite exercises and simple notes to a friend offering help with homework, building on the speaking practice from today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>